  "response": "The most likely cause is `direct_date` in `src/billing.ts`: seven wall-clock reads, including five `Date.now()` calls. If `generateInvoice` reads time repeatedly while a test crosses midnight, a month boundary, or another cutoff, one invocation can observe two different dates and produce inconsistent billing results.\n\nFix by injecting a clock and capturing one timestamp at the operation boundary:\n\n```ts\ninterface Clock {\n  now(): number;\n}\n\nconst systemClock: Clock = {\n  now: () => Date.now(),\n};\n\nfunction generateInvoice(\n  user: User,\n  clock: Clock = systemClock,\n): Invoice {\n  const now = clock.now();\n  const currentDate = new Date(now);\n\n  // Use `now` and `currentDate` throughout this invocation.\n}\n```\n\nTests should pass a fixed clock and include cases immediately before, exactly at, and immediately after the billing boundary:\n\n```ts\nconst clock = {\n  now: () => Date.parse(\"2026-02-01T00:00:00.000Z\"),\n};\n```\n\nUse UTC consistently for billing cutoffs. The `mixed_utc_local_methods` finding in `src/date.ts` is a secondary suspect if `billing.ts` calls those helpers: mixing `getUTCFullYear()` with local `getMonth()` can assign an instant to different days or months depending on the host timezone.\n\nThe 204-line `generateInvoice` increases the chance that one of its seven clock reads is missed during a patch, so extracting date/cutoff calculation into a pure helper would make the fix safer. The weak billing tests should also be strengthened to assert exact invoice dates, billing period, and totals—not merely that an invoice exists.",
